# Rotation for the sensorgnome VAH telemetry stream, which sensorgnome.service
# writes via StandardOutput=append:. systemd never rotates an append: target, so
# without this file the log grows unbounded on a 15 GB card. ~17 MB/day observed.
#
# copytruncate is REQUIRED here, not a stylistic choice. systemd holds this file
# open and never reopens it, so the default rename-then-create rotation would leave
# systemd writing into the renamed inode forever: the "current" log would stay empty
# and the rotated file would keep growing. copytruncate keeps that descriptor valid,
# and because systemd opens it O_APPEND, writes resume at offset 0 after the truncate
# instead of leaving a sparse hole.
#
# Trade-off accepted: lines written during the copy window can be lost. That is fine
# for 6-lines-per-10s telemetry and is the standard cost of copytruncate.
#
# daily + maxsize (not `size`): `size` alone REPLACES the time interval, whereas
# maxsize means "daily, but sooner if it exceeds this" — the burst guard we want.
/var/log/ctt-sensorgnome.log {
    daily
    maxsize 20M
    rotate 7
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
    su root root
}
